<!--  
treecity-revision: v10.8

v10.8 surface-safe ST6 correction (enhanced guard patch 2026-08-12)
  Diagnostic scenario 1 identified subtype 6 as the first root escaping above z=0.
  ST6 emergence angle is reduced and its directional flexibility modestly increased;
  all subtype-2 reference parameters and the mature hierarchy remain unchanged.

v10.8 (natural radial plate + mature hierarchy)
  Keeps subtype 2 shallow, limits subtype 6 to outward exploration, increases
  local fine/absorbing branching around the structural axes, and preserves
  sparse distributed sinkers/deep sinkers for utility conflicts. Subtype-1
  branch spacing is tightened toward 10-12 first-order structural laterals.

v10.7 (exploratory-depth correction + stronger length heterogeneity)
  Subtype 6 remains a shallow exploratory lateral: its emergence is closer to
  horizontal and the Python engine adds a directional return to its depth band.
  Subtype-2 elongation rate now has per-root deviation, because lmax
  heterogeneity alone cannot create unequal lengths while young roots are still
  far below lmax. The lmax deviation is also increased moderately because the
  realised v10.6 structural length CV remained below the 0.15 quality floor.

v10.5 (relief and heterogeneity)
  v10.4 fixed the clumping and the waviness. It then overshot: every structural
  lateral aimed at the same depth and grew to nearly the same length, so the
  age-60 plate rendered as a flat twelve-spoke wheel. The engine now staggers
  each lateral's target depth and lets it undulate; this file supplies the
  other half, length heterogeneity.

  * subtype 2 lmax deviation 70 -> 150. Twelve laterals of equal length is the
    single clearest sign of a synthetic root system. A real spruce plate is
    dominated by a few long laterals with several much shorter ones. With
    lmax = 500 dev 150 the realised maxima span roughly 250-750 cm.
  * subtype 6 lmax deviation 25 -> 55, for the same reason one level down.
  * subtype 4 (sinkers) raised from 5% to 7% of structural branch points and
    lengthened, because the sinkers are the mechanism of deep pipe conflict and
    were barely visible in the v10.4 render.

  The revision marker above is checked by validate_root_xml(). It exists
  because the numeric limits alone cannot tell an older parameter file from
  this one: the v10.3 and v10.4 files both satisfy every limit and would grow
  the wrong architecture silently.

Picea abies calibrated infrastructure-conflict architecture v10.4

Designed for TreeCity simulations from 1 to 60 years.
The architecture is dominated by 10-12 shallow structural laterals that
continue expanding gradually between ages 15 and 60. Distributed sinkers and
rare deep continuations are retained for conflicts below the main root plate.

WHY THIS FILE CHANGED IN v10.4 (root naturalness)
  The v10.3 plate was radial at the collar but still looked random further out.
  Two mechanical causes remained, both retuned here (the directional half of the
  fix lives in DEFAULT_ARCHITECTURE in urban_root_growth_predictor_v10_2.py):
    * Woody roots decided direction too often and too noisily. Structural
      subtypes (1, 2, 4, 6, 7) now use lower tropismS and more tropismN trials,
      and exploratory laterals (6) also use a larger dx, so they run straight.
    * Fine and absorbing roots (3, 5) curled into closed loops. Their tropismS
      is reduced and dx slightly raised so they stay flexible without looping.
  r, lmax and ln on subtype 2 are unchanged; the generator still owns them.

WHY THIS FILE CHANGED IN v10.3
  The v9.7 architecture produced a tangled, directionless plate. Two causes,
  neither of which can be fixed by XML alone:

  1. Azimuth. CPlantBox sets the radial emergence angle of EVERY root with
     "beta = 2 * M_PI * plant.lock()->rand()" (Root::Root in Root.cpp). With
     only ~11 structural laterals, uniform random azimuths clump several roots
     into one quadrant and leave 90-degree wedges empty. Real Picea abies
     leaves the collar in a roughly even radial fan.
  2. Direction memory. Plagiotropism (tropismT=0) only constrains the VERTICAL
     component of the heading; the horizontal direction is unconstrained and
     performs a random walk of one deflection per dx. Over a 500 cm lateral at
     dx=1.0 that is 500 independent turns, which is the curling spaghetti.

  Both are fixed in Python by PiceaStructuralTropism in
  urban_root_growth_predictor_v10_2.py, attached AFTER plant.initialize()
  because Plant::initCallbacks() overwrites f_tf during initialize().

  What this XML contributes is the mechanical half of the fix:
    * dx is much larger on structural roots. dx is the distance between
      direction decisions, so it sets root stiffness directly. Structural
      spruce roots are woody and near-straight, so they decide rarely
      (dx = 3.0 cm) while absorbing roots stay flexible (dx = 0.4 cm).
    * tropismS (sigma, the angular standard deviation per decision) is reduced
      by roughly an order of magnitude on structural roots and left high on
      fine roots, where waviness is real.
    * tropismN (number of trial directions per decision) is raised on
      structural roots. More trials means the chosen direction is closer to the
      tropism optimum, which straightens the root; it does NOT add randomness.
    * Exploratory subtype 6 is less abundant and shorter. At 18% off every
      structural branch point it formed a second, competing network that read
      as tangle rather than architecture.

  tropismS / tropismN here are read by PiceaStructuralTropism as its own
  parameters, so this file stays the single source of truth for both.
  validate_root_xml() enforces both the tropismS ceilings and the new dx
  minimums, so these values cannot silently drift back.

CPlantBox notes
  tropismT=0 is plagiotropism, not "tropism off".
  Small tropismS values and larger dx values prevent circular/wavy roots.
  Subtype-2 r, lmax and ln must stay identical to the 'reference' entry of
  ROOT_CONFIGS in the generator; check_xml_matches_root_configs() enforces it.

Subtype mapping:
  1 = short juvenile primary axis
  2 = structural plate lateral
  3 = fine transport root
  4 = structural sinker for subsurface utility conflicts
  5 = absorbing short root
  6 = exploratory shallow lateral
  7 = rare deep sub-sinker
  -->
